   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi John,<div><br></div><div>That makes sense! Thanks so much for your reply- I will look into using pcountopen or gpcount.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Kate</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Tuesday, November 21, 2017 at 8:52:46 PM UTC-7, John Clare wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Kate,<div><br></div><div>The N-mixture model fit using the function pcount assumes closure--in other words, N (the number of birds at a site) is treated as having a specific and fixed value over the duration of the survey. This assumption is almost definitely not true over three years of sampling, and maybe not true across even a single season (bird movement induces heterogeneity in # birds available for detection, i.e., N at a specific time may vary).  Ignoring this heterogeneity will certainly bias p and lambda, and I&#39;d guess your estimates are further wonky because of the null model structure (i.e., p and and N are likely to vary spatially regardless of the temporal sampling nature). The Applied Hierarchical Modeling book (v1, Kery and Royle) touches on this in greater detail.  You might look into pcountopen (aka Dail-Madsen models) or gpcount (no informal model name, but following Chandler et al. 2011 regarding temporary emigration) as alternatives. </div><div><br></div><div>--John</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
